# ---------------------------------------------------------------- to speak every language I meet (U R, cycle s2)

== lost for words in a strange town
tier: life event
aim: to speak every language I meet
aim_role: spark
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature elder
age: 14-80
per_year: 0 .3 .5 .4 .4 .3
tone: mixed
life: travel, learning
horizon: week
roles: friend, teacher, a stallholder, a family at the next table
earth: a town where nobody speaks a word one knows, after the last bus has gone
tribal: a fire past the last of the clan's trading places, where the people speak a tongue nobody back home has heard
magic: a land of the realm beyond the mountain pass, where the speech is old and the inn sign means nothing
scene: {N} is in a town where nobody speaks a word {N} knows, the last bus has gone, and the woman at the stall is asking something, kindly and fast.
scene W: {N} has a phrasebook and intends to use it properly, page by page.
scene U: {N} has noticed that the same three sounds keep coming back, and is sure they mean something.
scene B: {N} needs a bed for the night at a fair price, and knows the price goes up for people who cannot ask.
scene R: {N} is lost, a little scared, and more alive than in months.
scene G: {N} keeps watching how the families at the next table talk, and wants to be let in.
scene tribal: {N} has walked past the last of the clan's trading places, and the people at this fire speak a tongue nobody back home has heard.
scene magic: {N} has come through the mountain pass into a land where the speech is old and strange, and the inn sign means nothing.
- W | find the right page of the phrasebook and ask, word by careful word | v: conformity, security | foot: lesson manners need few words | chance: 70
- U | point, listen hard, and repeat every word back until you have ten | v: self-direction, achievement | dream: test | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 60
- B | haggle for a room with numbers on your fingers, and win | v: power, achievement | foot: means money+.03 | chance: 55
- R | follow the stallholder's waving hands wherever they lead | v: stimulation, self-direction | dream: test | foot: person friend | chance: 60
- G | sit with the family at the next table until they make room | v: benevolence, tradition | foot: means ties+.04 | chance: 70
- W>B | find someone official who speaks a little of your tongue | v: conformity, security | foot: person go-between | chance: 65
- U>R | stay a month in the town and learn the speech from the street up | v: self-direction, stimulation | foot: means time-.08 | chance: 55
- B>G | pay a local youngster to be guide and teacher for the week | v: power, benevolence | foot: person teacher | chance: 70
- R>W | sing the one song you both know, and let the whole stall join in | v: stimulation, benevolence | foot: lesson songs need no translation | chance: 65
- G>U | take the first bus home in the morning, and stay home | v: security, tradition | dream: drop | foot: mark came home | chance: 90
works: By the end of the week {N} can order bread, argue the price and tell a joke that nearly works.
works: The stallholder laughs at {Ns} accent, then teaches {N} ten words over tea.
fails: Every try comes out wrong, and {N} spends a cold night in the bus shelter.
fails: {N} learns one word, and it turns out to be a rude one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- to fall for a mind as quick as mine (U R, cycle s3)

== the argument that turns into something else
tier: life event
aim: to fall for a mind as quick as mine
aim_role: spark
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 16-65
per_year: 0 .2 .5 .4 .2 0
tone: joy
life: love, friends
horizon: week
roles: prospect, friend, ex, rival
earth: an argument at a party about something that does not matter, that neither side wants to end
tribal: a game of riddles at the gathering of the clans, and someone who answers every one with a harder one
magic: three evenings in the tower library, disagreeing about one line of an old book
scene: At a party {N} and {prospect} have been arguing for an hour about something that does not matter, and neither of them wants to stop.
scene W: {N} keeps the argument fair, and notices that {prospect} does too.
scene U: Every time {N} thinks the point is won, {prospect} finds a new angle, and {N} has never enjoyed losing so much.
scene B: {N} wants to win, and wants {prospect} to see it.
scene R: {Ns} heart is going faster than the argument deserves.
scene G: {N} keeps noticing how {prospect} listens, really listens, before answering.
scene tribal: At the gathering of the clans, {prospect} has answered every one of {Ns} riddles with a harder one, and the fire is burning low.
scene magic: In the tower library, {prospect} has disagreed with {N} about one line of an old book for three evenings running.
- W | concede the good point graciously, and ask to carry on tomorrow | v: benevolence, conformity | foot: lesson a good loser is remembered | chance: 75
- U | push the argument somewhere neither of you has been before | v: self-direction, stimulation | dream: test | bond: prospect +.1 | chance: 65
- B | win the argument outright, and watch {prospect} take it | v: power, achievement | foot: means ties-.03 | chance: 55
- R | say out loud that this is the best evening in years | v: stimulation, hedonism | dream: seal | title: girlfriend or boyfriend | chance: 50
- G | walk {prospect} home, and keep talking at the gate | v: benevolence, security | foot: person prospect | chance: 75
- W>R | swap numbers, and set a date to finish the argument properly | v: conformity, stimulation | foot: door kept your word | chance: 80
- U>G | lend {prospect} the book that started it, with notes in the margins | v: self-direction, benevolence | foot: door made a friend | chance: 80
- B>W | take {friend} aside and ask what they know about {prospect} | v: power, security | foot: person go-between | chance: 70
- R>U | slip out of the party with {prospect} to argue somewhere quieter | v: stimulation, self-direction | foot: mark took a wild risk | chance: 60
- G>B | step back, and leave {prospect} to {friend}, who saw them first | v: benevolence, security | dream: drop | bond: friend +.1 | chance: 85
works: By the end of the night they have agreed on nothing at all, and {N} has {prospect}'s number.
works: The argument goes on for weeks, over coffee and long walks, and stops being an argument.
fails: {prospect} goes home with someone who agreed with everything.
fails: The point is won, and the evening is lost.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- to raise children who question everything (U R, cycle s4)

== the night of a hundred whys
tier: life event
aim: to raise children who question everything
aim_role: spark
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 20-60
needs: children
per_year: 0 0 .4 .5 .3 0
tone: joy
life: children, family
horizon: moment
roles: child, partner, parent, grandparent
earth: a child's why, long past bedtime, and then forty more
tribal: a child under the stars asking why the moon follows the band when it moves camp
magic: a child asking why the tower's lights never go out, and why the masters lock their books
scene: It is long past bedtime, and {child} has asked why the sky goes dark, and then why, and then why again, forty times, and {N} is running out of answers.
scene W: {N} knows bedtime matters, and so does the question.
scene U: {N} is delighted, and a little worried about question forty-one.
scene B: {N} wants {child} to be the sharpest one in the class, and this is how it starts.
scene R: {N} would happily stay up all night with this, bedtime or not.
scene G: {N} remembers asking {parent} the same things, and being told to hush.
scene tribal: Under the stars {child} has asked why the moon follows the band when it moves camp, and why, and why again.
scene magic: {child} has asked why the tower's lights never go out, and why the masters keep their books locked, and why again.
- W | answer the first three, and promise to look up the rest tomorrow | v: conformity, benevolence | foot: door kept your word | chance: 80
- U | answer each why with a question that lets {child} work it out | v: self-direction, benevolence | dream: test | bond: child +.1 | chance: 65
- B | enter {child} for the junior quiz team at the town library | v: achievement, power | foot: place learn.keeping | chance: 70
- R | throw open the window and look at the stars together until midnight | v: stimulation, hedonism | dream: test | foot: lesson wonder is worth lost sleep | chance: 85
- G | ring {parent} so that {child} can ask them too | v: tradition, benevolence | bond: parent +.1 | chance: 80
- W>G | make it a family rule: every question gets its turn at dinner | v: conformity, benevolence | foot: means time-.04 | chance: 75
- U>W | buy a proper encyclopedia and look things up together, every night | v: self-direction, conformity | foot: means money-.04 | chance: 85
- B>U | tell {child} to put the question to the teacher tomorrow, and see what happens | v: power, self-direction | foot: door defied an authority | chance: 55
- R>B | build a rocket in the garden at the weekend to find out | v: stimulation, achievement | foot: mark learned a skill | chance: 60
- G>R | tell {child} it is a mystery, and some things are allowed to stay one | v: tradition, stimulation | dream: drop | foot: lesson a mystery can be kept | chance: 85
works: Somewhere around the thirtieth why, {child} answers one alone, and is very pleased about it.
works: {child} falls asleep in the middle of a question, and asks the next one at breakfast.
fails: {N} runs out of answers and snaps, and {child} goes quiet in a way that lasts.
fails: The questions go round in a circle, and everyone goes to bed cross.
